A||N||7||grinthock||ATF Change Procedure - Help? ||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||11-05-2001||11:51 PM||||Looking for the procedure to change my tranny fluid, never done it, EVER, engine's interest me, tranny's scare me.<P>I want to install a shift kit and fix my TCC Solonoid, so any help is GREAT!<P>Pictures would be even better but now I think I'm pushing it<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||11-06-2001||06:51 AM||||Hey Grin!<BR>Go down to NAPA and buy a tranny filter kit, it comes with a new gasket...<BR>Drop the pan and replace the filter, clean the pan and reinstall...<BR>Refill the ATF and away you go!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||11-06-2001||07:12 AM||||can I have a little more description?||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||11-06-2001||10:10 AM||||remove the 13mm bolts all the way around the transmission pan. (wear an old tee shirt/messy) allow all the fluid to run/drain out. remove old filter (just pull straight down) likely the filter gasket/o ring will drop with it. clean pan and install new filter and o ring gasket. new pan gasket and tighten pan. pan bolts are more snug than tight especially if you bought a cork gasket. refill trans through dipstick pipe with 5.5L of dextron fluid and away you go.<P>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000004||rodmcneill||11-06-2001||01:44 PM||||If you don't drop the pan straight down but leave a couple of loosened bolts in a corner of the pan you can use the pan kinda as a funnel and try to direct the fluid into your container. It's still a messy job. My local tranny shop used to have fluid change special $29.99 and that included filter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||grinthock||11-08-2001||01:44 AM||||Sorry guys been busy so this is a little BUMP!  <P><BR>So while I change the fluid and filter which I have a handle on now, how do I install a shift kit?<BR> That's done at the same time right?  <P>Also where is the TCC Solonoid, I will do that at the same time too.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||11-08-2001||09:23 AM||||Since there's no drain plug on the trans pan, this can be a messy job.<P>I use a "Topside Oil Changer" (looks like a gas can and has a vacuum pump and tubing) to suck the trans fluid out the dipstick tube so I can remove the pan without making a mess.<P>The Topside was $20 from Advance Auto as I recall. It also works great for changing small engine oil like in a lawnmower.||||reg||1
Z||000007||AkursedX||11-08-2001||11:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Sorry guys been busy so this is a little BUMP!  <P><BR>So while I change the fluid and filter which I have a handle on now, how do I install a shift kit?<BR> That's done at the same time right?  <P>Also where is the TCC Solonoid, I will do that at the same time too.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The shift kit and the TCC are located in the same place, behind the transmission cover that is on the driver's side of the tranny.  The cover that is right near where the TCC  plug goes in to.<P>You will have to take out the wheelwell and work through there.  It looks fairly easy to do, but time consuming as well.  <BR><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
